Z||000026||DRH||06-14-2001||11:31 PM||||Personally, I wouldn't buy a very low miles car because I know I couldn't keep it that way. I'm glad that some people do though so everybody else will have a pristine example to look at somewhere down the road. Restored is great, but not quite the same. <P>As someone else pointed out... don't think of it as wasting but as saving for later. Visiting an auto museum can give you an appreciation for what some of these people are trying to do in concept anyway. A lot of the cars in museums had no special value at the time, but are really great to look at now because they are one of only a few left. Think of your grandkids and beyond.<P><BR>Edited because I forgot to say congrats on your find!!!<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||RobMich||06-15-2001||11:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RossT:<BR><B>Rob, Did you every sell your 87gt??<P>Nice find!  That must be the one that the guy in Alaska was selling.  The price he was asking in Hemmings was very good!!  You did well. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yup, sold my '87 to a nice gentleman in Indiana.  I was very lucky, the price even included shipping in a covered container via barge from Alaska.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000028||danny boy||06-16-2001||03:15 AM|||| The first Fiero I ever saw was an '87 GT. Blue. It was the most beautiful thing I had ever seen. The owner lived behind me on the next street over. I used to just sit on the curb and stare at it for hours.<P>  Or maybe it just seemed like it because I was 11 years old.<BR> <BR> Anyway, Thats what started me in this whole mess. If I could find a very low mile 87 GT thats all origional and was blue I would snatch it up(God and Issa willing) to keep forever.<BR> Oh, I'd keep my current GT very driven and very abused.And when it's dead my coupe is next.<BR>  ||||reg||1
